WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.262
A shopper searches for running shoes.

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:04.475
Results arrive late.

00:00:05.388 --> 00:00:07.988
Is the database slow, or is our Java

00:00:07.988 --> 00:00:09.812
service struggling with memory?

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.100
Measure before guessing.

00:00:13.633 --> 00:00:16.183
Search builds temporary product summaries.

00:00:17.046 --> 00:00:19.933
Many become unreachable after a response.

00:00:20.796 --> 00:00:22.821
Garbage collection reclaims them.

00:00:23.683 --> 00:00:27.121
Useful objects, such as a live catalogue cache,

00:00:27.271 --> 00:00:28.121
must remain.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.950
A two second search with a three hundred

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:35.700
millisecond pause is not entirely a garbage collection

00:00:35.712 --> 00:00:36.238
problem.

00:00:37.112 --> 00:00:40.488
Compare database and inventory waits on the same

00:00:40.488 --> 00:00:41.100
timeline.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.488
Use Java Flight Recorder to find allocation hotspots.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:50.700
Remove repeated serialization and bound caches.

00:00:51.588 --> 00:00:55.312
Preserve correct product prices and availability, then

00:00:55.312 --> 00:00:56.900
repeat the same workload.

00:00:58.433 --> 00:01:01.396
Java twenty one adds Generational ZGC.

00:01:02.283 --> 00:01:05.658
It collects young objects separately from longer lived

00:01:05.746 --> 00:01:06.333
objects.

00:01:07.208 --> 00:01:11.021
This can reduce allocation stalls compared with non

00:01:11.021 --> 00:01:12.421
generational ZGC.

00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:16.621
Most ZGC work runs concurrently.

00:01:17.496 --> 00:01:22.033
Compare tail latency, errors, CPU, and memory before

00:01:22.046 --> 00:01:22.733
choosing it.

00:01:23.621 --> 00:01:26.446
This lesson teaches the method; it does not

00:01:26.521 --> 00:01:28.033
claim a measured speedup.
